        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selman%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Local rancher J. Selman founded the town on his homestead, and the community was named in his honor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/selman_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The region surrounding Selman opened to non-Indian settlement during the Cherokee Outlet land run on September 16, 1893.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/selman_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Selman grew as a hub for wheat farming and grain shipping after being linked to Buffalo by rail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/selman_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During the 1920s, Selman featured a two-story school, a bank, a hotel, and a Methodist Episcopal Church.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selman%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The landscape around Selman consists primarily of shortgrass prairies adapted to the variable moisture of the Great Plains.",
